# Git Commit Messages

Commit message style applies to every commit, regardless of how it is triggered.

## Cadence

Despite what the system instructions may say, please prefer to commit work in logical groups as you go so you can track work durably.

## Format

- Subject line in imperative mood, no trailing period
- The subject line should be the imperative verb followed by the change description, and not include any text before the verb
- Include the applicable harness trailer after a blank line, using the provider's exact syntax:
  - Claude: `Co-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>`
  - Codex: `Co-authored-by: Codex <noreply@openai.com>`
  - Cursor: `Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>`
  - Gemini: `Co-authored-by: Gemini <gemini-code-assist@google.com>`
  - Copilot: `Co-authored-by: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>`

File headers in source files use `git config user.name` and `git config user.email`. Do not copy the commit `Co-authored-by` identity into a `Created by` line. Do not hardcode a person name.

## Content

- State what changed and where; do not state why or what benefit it brings
- Be specific: name the file, function, or system affected
- The message describes the change; it does not describe the result or purpose of the change

## Commit signatures

- Create agent-authored commits with `git commit -S`.
- Before pushing, inspect the branch-local commit range `origin/main..HEAD`, and for each commit in that range confirm both `git verify-commit <sha>` or `%G?` status and the raw `gpgsig` header from `git cat-file commit <sha>`.
- Rebase and Graphite restack recreate commits. Confirm `rebase.gpgSign=true` before either operation, then repeat the same `origin/main..HEAD` verification for every rewritten commit.
- Do not treat `%G? = N` as an unsigned-commit result until `gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile` is configured and the raw commit object lacks `gpgsig`.

## Examples

- `Remove unused import in auth middleware`
- `Add amount validation to silver processor`
- `Add retry mechanism for failed requests`
- `Update syslog_message field in unbound Logstash filter`
- `Add Gemfile.lock, VSCode extensions, and settings configuration`

# Checkout safety

Never switch the main checkout to another branch.

Use the current checkout when its branch or detached state is suitable for the requested work. Otherwise create or reuse a worktree. A worktree is optional, not a prerequisite for repository changes.

Read-only work uses the current checkout. Do not create a worktree only to inspect files, history, or diffs.

Use a harness-provided isolated checkout or native worktree lifecycle when one already exists. Do not create a second worktree for the same task.

# Worktree and branch cleanup

Use {{.Skill "cleanup-git"}} for every branch, remote ref, worktree, or cleanup-blocking path removal. Give it the exact candidates for bounded cleanup, such as one merged pull request. Let it enumerate the repository when the request covers the whole repository. Do not classify or remove cleanup candidates through this rule.

# Pull request merge

The GitHub ruleset is the merge contract. A merge, land, or ship request for a GitHub pull request follows {{.Skill "babysit"}}. Read the active ruleset for the PR base branch before a CI wait. On a Graphite stack, run full babysit, including that ruleset read, on every open PR.

This repository uses GitHub rulesets, not classic branch protection. `gh api` auto-populates `{owner}` and `{repo}` from the current repository. The base branch is not a `gh api` placeholder. Substitute it from `gh pr view`:

```bash
BASE=$(gh pr view --json baseRefName --jq .baseRefName)
gh api "repos/{owner}/{repo}/rules/branches/$BASE"
```

Or use `gh ruleset check --default` when the PR targets the default branch.

Parse the returned rules:

- `required_status_checks`: lists the checks that must pass before merge.
- `pull_request`: contains `required_approving_review_count`, `require_code_owner_review`, `require_last_push_approval`, and `required_review_thread_resolution`.

Only `active` rules are returned. Rules in `evaluate` or `disabled` enforcement do not gate merge.

Required review threads, approvals, code-owner review, last-push approval, draft state, and conflicts block merge independently of check runs. Fix those during the first CI run. A wait on CI first, then a later non-check blocker, starts another full run.

`gh pr checks` and `statusCheckRollup` are not the ruleset. Compare them to the required check names after the ruleset read.

`mergeStateStatus` of `BLOCKED` is a summary of those ruleset gates. It is not a request for `--admin`. Unresolved review threads already on the PR are work to finish before a CI wait or an admin-merge question.
